description:
`spline2_create()` in `R/spline_helpers.R` (shared by `step_bs`, `step_ns`, `step_spline_b`, and other spline steps built on it) swaps the error text for the two `complete_set` branches:

```r
if (.fn != "bernsteinPoly" && isTRUE(degree > (df - complete_set))) {
  if (complete_set) {
    cli::cli_abort(
      "{.arg degree} ({degree}) must be less than to {.arg deg_free} \\
      ({df}) when {.code complete_set = FALSE}.",
      call = call
    )
  } else {
    cli::cli_abort(
      "{.arg degree} ({degree}) must be less than or equal to {.arg deg_free} \\
      ({df}) when {.code complete_set = TRUE}.",
      call = call
    )
  }
}
```

When `complete_set` is actually `TRUE`, the message tells the user the error is about `complete_set = FALSE`, and vice versa, which is confusing when debugging.

Suggested fix: swap the two message bodies so the `complete_set` branch reports `complete_set = TRUE` and the `else` branch reports `complete_set = FALSE`.

right now we use the following idium a lot. This is fine and all, but it isn't tested 100%, and once we add sparse tibble support it would be helpful if this is done using a robust helper function everywhere.

```r
  if (!is_tibble(data)) {
    data <- as_tibble(data)
  }
```

Currently we need `tidy` function to to inspect recipe steps.
However, I think the name is not intuitive for several reasons

- The name does not contain `step`
    - This also make IDE to suggest the proper function when user guess the function contains `step` as its name.
- Too many `tidy` methods in the help

I'd suggest something like below.
I also add `.tidy` argument so that user can get raw result from the step when `.tidy = FALSE`.

```r
inspect_step <- function(x, number = NA, id = NA, ..., .tidy = TRUE) {
  if (.tidy) {
    return(tidy(x, number, id, ...)
  }
  if (!is.na(number)) {
    return(x$steps[[number]])
  }
  x$steps[[id]]
}
```

For [this project](https://github.com/orgs/tidymodels/projects/14), the idea is to have steps that can be used to process text data (contained in a new package). I've made placeholders in that project for some obvious processing candidates. 

@EmilHvitfeldt has volunteered to get started. Perhaps @juliasilge, @skeydan, and others might have some suggestions and opinions. I'd be happy to include tensorflow methods for text processing too. 

I can add anyone interested in helping to the project page. We can use this issue to discuss ideas and kick around implementation questions.
